WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:01.709
Speaker 1: , OK.

00:00:01.709 --> 00:00:08.780
Senate Democrats are to propose federal cannabis
decriminalization.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:16.560
For many people, one of the top issues of
the Joe Biden campaign was the full decriminalization

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:22.789
of cannabis, of marijuana at the federal level
as a starting point for hopefully eventual

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:25.510
rescheduling, further legalization.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.919
Remember, decriminalization and legalization
are two different things, and we'll talk about

00:00:29.919 --> 00:00:32.619
the impact of that actually on tomorrow's
program.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:37.270
So it's not legalization that we're talking
about, but it is still a really, really big

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.780
deal.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:45.550
CNBC reporting Senate Majority Leader Chuck
Schumer proposed legislation to legalize marijuana

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.320
at the federal level.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.460
The Cannabis Administration and Opportunity
Act would remove marijuana from the Controlled

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.400
Substances Act and introduce regulations to
tax cannabis products.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.800
Now, even the article is not exactly right
because the proposal is for decriminalize,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:03.010
which is in the headline.

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:04.989
But then the point says Legalize.

00:01:04.989 --> 00:01:07.020
That's not actually what the proposal is.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.830
It would also expunge federal records of nonviolent
cannabis offenders.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.680
This is a huge deal.

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:16.710
This was proposed yesterday.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:25.490
And the idea here is start laying the groundwork
to ease legislation that while it may not

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:32.720
in the way that it is written, disproportionately
target poor people and people of color in

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:33.720
practice.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.570
That's what the war on drugs has done.

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.159
And we know because we've heard from the people
who were involved in the creation of the current

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:45.310
infrastructure we have around the criminality
of drugs, that that was actually the goal.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:51.210
The war on drugs was a I mean, I sort of want
to say racially tinged, but that's not really

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:52.550
strong enough.

00:01:52.550 --> 00:01:57.800
It was a racially targeted reaction to the
civil rights movement that started in the

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:04.030
late 50s and sort of made more progress during
the 1960s in the United States.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.769
The proposal, the Cannabis Administration
and Opportunity Act would remove marijuana

00:02:08.769 --> 00:02:14.099
from the Controlled Substances Act and introduce
regulations to cannabis products.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:19.040
The proposal would expunge federal records
of nonviolent cannabis offenders and allow

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.290
people serving time in federal prison and
importantly, federal, not state, for nonviolent

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.200
marijuana crimes to petition a court for resentencing.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:34.889
The plan would also create an opportunity
trust fund from new cannabis tax revenue in

00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:41.319
order to invest in programs for communities
most affected by the, quote, failed war on

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:44.109
drugs, according to a draft of the bill.

00:02:44.109 --> 00:02:47.749
Now, what what is the probability of this
becoming law?

00:02:47.749 --> 00:02:53.231
The plan to decriminalize marijuana will likely
face a contentious battle in the Senate, as

00:02:53.231 --> 00:02:58.950
it's generally opposed by Republicans and
some moderate Democrats and has not received

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.480
an endorsement from President Joe Biden.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:07.279
Schumer will require 60 votes, including at
least 10 GOP votes, to pass it through the

00:03:07.279 --> 00:03:10.870
Senate in terms of what we've seen on legalization
and.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:17.340
Criminalization so far, so far, 37 states
and D.C. have legalized medical cannabis in

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:24.059
the 18 states and D.C. have legalized recreational
use, but it is still illegal under federal

00:03:24.059 --> 00:03:25.059
law.

00:03:25.059 --> 00:03:29.680
Public polling shows that nearly 70 percent
of Americans support legalizing the drug.

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.290
So there's a bunch of stories here.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.870
One story is, is Joe Biden going to, after
what he talked about during his campaign,

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.530
come out and endorse this particular bill?

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:45.329
As many of you know, in politics, often you
campaign on supporting an idea and then when

00:03:45.329 --> 00:03:50.390
a bill is put forth, that would make that
idea a reality.

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:56.389
Oftentimes, politicians will say, well, this
particular bill, as written, has this problem,

00:03:56.389 --> 00:04:01.339
has that problem and remains to be seen whether
where Joe Biden is going to land on this particular

00:04:01.339 --> 00:04:02.339
bill.

00:04:02.339 --> 00:04:08.449
Number two, we have talked increasingly over
the years about how in the United States the

00:04:08.449 --> 00:04:16.100
policy desires of the American people as a
whole often don't get made a reality.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.769
And this is this is for a number of different
reasons.

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:23.890
One reason is that our elected officials are
far more conservative than the population

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:24.890
at large.

00:04:24.890 --> 00:04:29.979
If you look at sort of the average political
placement on the spectrum of at the federal

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:35.159
level, senators and members of Congress, and
you compare their their positions on policy

00:04:35.159 --> 00:04:36.770
to those of the American people.

00:04:36.770 --> 00:04:39.740
For example, 70 percent support legalization.

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:43.870
And yet we don't even know that we can get
60 percent of the Senate to say, yeah, I'll

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.349
vote for this bill.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.319
That's one issue that we've talked about as
well.

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:50.300
So there's a number of different stories here.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:59.199
And we know that corporations and elites and
the wealthy have outsized influence on what

00:04:59.199 --> 00:05:03.620
actually becomes law and what the legislative
priorities are relative to just the average

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.620
person.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:08.979
One stunning example of this is, of course,
after the Sandy Hook school shooting during

00:05:08.979 --> 00:05:13.520
Barack Obama's presidency, which was now many
I don't even know the number of years.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.889
It's way more it feels way more recent than
than it actually is.

00:05:16.889 --> 00:05:20.389
There was like eighty eight, 90 percent support
for what's called universal background checks

00:05:20.389 --> 00:05:22.900
among the American people in the United States.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:26.720
And yet Democrats who at the time controlled
the White House, the House and the Senate

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.360
were not able to get universal background
checks made into law.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:33.470
And there's a ton of different reasons for
why that is, and all of them corrosive to

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.620
the idea of a representative democracy.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:42.449
But that's one example, the American people's
view now on cannabis, at minimum, decriminalization

00:05:42.449 --> 00:05:43.720
is another example.

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:47.800
Here's an opportunity good for Chuck Schumer
for putting forth the bill, but we will see

